<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joachim Ernst, Duke of Anhalt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joachim Ernst was the last Duke of Anhalt, succeeding as a minor in September 1918. His regent abdicated on his behalf in November 1918 during the German Revolution, and the duchy became the Free State of Anhalt. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he was arrested by the Soviet authorities and died in a camp at Buchenwald in 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joachim-ernst-duke-of-anhalt.jpg" alt="Joachim Ernst, Duke of Anhalt" width="290" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. Hartmann, Hofphotograph, Dessau [Adolf Hartmann (1864–1923)] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joachim_Ernst_duke_of_Anhalt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joachim Ernst Wilhelm Karl Albrecht Leopold Friedrich Moritz Erdmann was born on 11 January 1901 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, then the capital of the duchy. He was the fourth child and eldest surviving son of Prince Eduard of Anhalt and Princess Louise Charlotte of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, and a grandson of Duke Friedrich I of Anhalt. He was the only German monarch born in the 20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He succeeded his father on 13 September 1918, with his uncle Prince Aribert of Anhalt as regent because of his age. Aribert abdicated in his name on 12 November 1918, so that Joachim Ernst was the last ruler of a constituent state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> to come to the throne before the German monarchies were abolished. The abdication ended the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which had lasted since the 11th century. The family kept Ballenstedt Castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz Mountains</a> as private property, and all his children were born there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joachim Ernst joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in 1939 but later became a critic of Nazi rule; arrested in the winter of 1944, he spent three months in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp">Dachau concentration camp</a>. He welcomed the fall of the regime and stayed in the Soviet occupation zone, believing his record as an anti-Nazi would protect him. On 31 August 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> arrested him in a general round-up of landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and seized his estate of 20,000 hectares. He died on 18 February 1947 in NKVD special camp Nr. 2, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp">Buchenwald concentration camp</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>.</p><p>His first marriage, to Elisabeth Strickrodt, a daughter of an opera singer, in 1927 ended in divorce in 1929 without children. In 1929 he married Editha Marwitz von Stephani, with whom he had five children. After his death the headship of the ducal house was disputed between his elder son, Prince Friedrich, and his brother, Prince Eugen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joachim Ernst, Duke of Anhalt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Joachim Ernst</b>, a minor, abdicated through his regent in November 1918, and the duchy became the Free State of Anhalt.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Joachim Ernst</b>'s regent abdicated in November 1918, and the Duchy of Anhalt became a free state, with a democratic constitution adopted in 1919 and Dessau as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-anhalt">Free State of Anhalt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in NKVD special camp Nr. 2, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Dessau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4826 371 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joachim Ernst, Duke of Anhalt"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5276 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5276 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.83,12.25&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
